            <ArticleTitle>2'-O-Methyl-5-hydroxymethylcytidine: A Second Oxidative Derivative of 5-Methylcytidine in RNA.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1766-1769</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/jacs.6b12180</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>5-Hydroxymethylcytidine (hm5C) was recently identified as a direct metabolite of m5C in RNA. We investigated the stability of hm5C in human cells using bio-isotopologues and LC-MS/HRMS. This has led to the discovery of a second oxidative metabolite of m5C in RNA, namely 2'-O-methyl-5-hydroxymethylcytidine (hm5Cm). Subsequent quantitative analysis of total RNA from higher organisms revealed varying levels and TET-independent formation of this new RNA modification.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
